There is an absolute constant $C<\infty$ such that, for every real-valued $f\in W^{1,1}(\mathbb R^2)$, the function $Mf$ is locally integrable, belongs to $W^{1,1}_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb R^2)$, and satisfies $$\label{eq:main}
 \int_{\mathbb R^2}|\nabla Mf|\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}x
 \le C\int_{\mathbb R^2}|\nabla f|\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}x.$$ In particular, its distributional derivatives are represented by globally integrable functions.

There is an absolute constant $C$ such that, for every real $g\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R^2)$ and $0<a\le b$ with $b/a$ a power of two, including one, $$\label{eq:intro-band}
 \int_{\mathbb R^2}|\nabla S_{a,b}g|\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}x
 \le C\int_{\mathbb R^2}|\nabla g|\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}x.$$ The constant is independent of $a$, $b$, and the number of dyadic radius scales between them.

Let $Q$ be a square of side length one and let $\Omega=\{y:\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}(y,Q)<10\}$. Suppose that, on $\Omega$, $$\label{eq:local-decomposition}
h=\sum_i v_i\sigma_i+\beta,
\qquad \beta=\sum_d\beta_d.$$ The sums are finite. The measures in the sums are finite, defined on $\mathbb R^2$, and absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure. Each $\sigma_i$ is nonnegative and each $v_i$ is a constant unit vector. For each $d$, write $m_d=|\beta_d|(\mathbb R^2)$ and assign a number $s_d\in(0,1]$. If $s_d<1$, assume that $\beta_d(\mathbb R^2)=0$ and that $\beta_d$ is supported in a ball of radius at most $C_s s_d$, where $C_s$ is fixed. Set $$S=\sum_i\sigma_i,
\qquad A=\sum_d s_d^\alpha m_d.$$ For $x\in Q$, define $$\begin{aligned}
\mathcal I(x)
&=\{r\in[1,2]:u_r(x)\ge u_t(x)
                 \text{ for every }t>0\text{ with }|t-r|\le1/4\},\\
\mathcal J(x)
&=\{r\in[1,2]:u_r(x)=\max_{1\le t\le2}u_t(x)\}\cup\{1,2\}.
\end{aligned}$$ Then the following estimates hold.*


1.  **Unfavorable directional gradients.* If $e$ is a unit vector and $e\cdot v_i\ge\kappa>0$ for all $i$, then $$\label{eq:local-direction}
    \int_Q\sup_{r\in\mathcal I(x)}
                      (-e\cdot\nabla u_r(x))_+\,dx\le C A.$$*


2.  **Band maxima and forced endpoints.* Without a condition on the directions $v_i$, $$\label{eq:local-size}
    \int_Q\sup_{r\in\mathcal J(x)}|\nabla u_r(x)|\,dx
        \le C\bigl(A+S(\mathbb R^2)\bigr).$$*


3.  **Differences of nearby averages.* We also have $$\label{eq:local-value}
    \int_Q\sup_{0<t,t'\le4}|u_t(x)-u_{t'}(x)|\,dx
        \le C\bigl(A+S(\mathbb R^2)\bigr).$$*

*Here $C$ depends only on $C_s$, and in the first estimate also on $\kappa$. A supremum of nonnegative quantities over an empty set is zero.

Let $x\in\mathbb R^2$, $r\in[1,2]$, $0<\delta\le1/4$, and $$D=\{y:\bigl||y-x|-r\bigr|\le\delta\}.$$ Let $h$ be a complex measure whose variation is finite on $B(x,3)$. Suppose that $K_0,K_1:\mathbb R^2\to[0,1]$ are Borel functions supported in $B(x,3)$, that $K_1-K_0$ is supported in $D$, and that $$\int K_i(y)\overline{y-x}\,dh(y)=0\qquad(i=0,1).$$ Set $P_i=\int K_i\,dh$. For every $z\in D$, $$\label{eq:local-affine-identity}
 P_1-P_0=\int
 \left(1-\frac{\overline{y-x}}{\overline{z-x}}\right)
 (K_1-K_0)(y)\,dh(y).$$ For every $0<\rho\le1$, this implies $$\begin{aligned}
\label{eq:local-affine-bound}
 |P_1-P_0|\le{}&2\rho\int_{D\cap B(z,\rho)}
                    |K_1-K_0|\,d|h|\notag\\
 &+10\int_{D\setminus B(z,\rho)}|K_1-K_0|\,d|h|.
\end{aligned}$$

Let $S$ and $\nu$ be finite positive Borel measures on $\mathbb R^2$, let $U>0$ and $T\ge0$, and suppose that $$0<\rho\le1,
\qquad 0<\delta\le\frac{\rho^2}{1600}.$$ Write $$D(x,r,\delta)=\{y:\bigl||y-x|-r\bigr|\le\delta\}.$$ There is a Borel set $E\subset\mathbb R^2$ such that $$\label{eq:shell-exception}
|E|\le C\left[
  \left(\frac{\nu(\mathbb R^2)}{U}\right)^2\frac{\delta}{\rho}
  +\frac{\nu(\mathbb R^2)}{U}\frac{\delta}{\rho^2}
           \left(1+\frac{T}{U}\right)\right],$$ and the following implication holds for every $x\notin E$ and every $r\in[1,2]$: if $$\label{eq:shell-hypotheses}
\nu(D(x,r,\delta))\ge U,
\qquad S(B(x,r-\delta))\le T,$$ then there is $z\in D(x,r,\delta)$ for which $$\label{eq:shell-cap}
(S+\nu)\bigl(D(x,r,\delta)\setminus B(z,\rho)\bigr)\le2U.$$ The constant in <a href="#eq:shell-exception" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:shell-exception">[eq:shell-exception]</a> is absolute. In particular, neither the exceptional set nor its bound involves a choice of $r$.

For every $c\in\mathcal C$ there are measures $\rho_s^c,\mu_{s,+}^c,\mu_{s,-}^c$ at all vertices $s\in V_l$, $1\le l\le m$, supported in $\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\phi_s$ and absolutely continuous, with $\rho_s^c$ of constant sign and $\mu_{s,\pm}^c$ nonnegative, such that $$\label{eq:prices-scalar-masses}
 \rho_s^c(\mathbb R^2)=c_s,\qquad |\rho_s^c|(\mathbb R^2)=|c_s|,
 \qquad
 \mu_{s,+}^c(\mathbb R^2)=\mu_{s,-}^c(\mathbb R^2)=d_s^c.$$ Put $\beta_s^c=\mu_{s,+}^c-\mu_{s,-}^c$. For every $q\in V_k$, $1\le k\le m$, the following is an identity of measures on the whole plane: $$\label{eq:prices-vertex-reconstruction}
 \phi_q\nu^c=\rho_q^c+
       \sum_{l=k}^m\sum_{s\in V_l}\lambda_{qs}\beta_s^c.$$ Only finitely many terms in this identity are nonzero, and $$\label{eq:prices-pair-properties}
 \beta_s^c(\mathbb R^2)=0,\qquad
 |\beta_s^c|(\mathbb R^2)\le2d_s^c,\qquad
 \mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\beta_s^c\subseteq\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\phi_s
       \subseteq\overline B(s,\sqrt2 r_l).$$

For $p\in V_k$, $1\le k\le m$, define $$D_{Q_p}=\sum_{c\in\mathcal C}
       \sum_{q\in\mathcal R(Q_p)}
       \sum_{l=k}^m2^{-\alpha(l-k)}
       \sum_{s\in V_l}\lambda_{qs}d_s^c,
 \qquad \alpha=\frac1{100}.
 \label{eq:prices-definition}$$ These nonnegative prices satisfy $$\sum_{k=1}^m\sum_{p\in V_k}D_{Q_p}
 \le\frac{41^2}{2(1-2^{-\alpha})}
           \sum_{c\in\mathcal C}\|c(\nabla g)\|_{L^1}
 \le C V.
 \label{eq:price-budget}$$ For every such cell $Q$ and every $n\in\mathcal N$, $$\begin{aligned}
 &\int_Q\sup_{t\in\mathcal I_k(x)}
       \sum_{\varepsilon\in\{+,-\}}
       (-e_\varepsilon(n)\cdot\nabla u_t(x))_+\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}x
   \le C\left(D_Q+
       \sum_{\substack{q\in\mathcal R(Q)\\(q,n)\text{ bad}}}a_q\right),
 \\
 &\int_Q\left[\sup_{t\in\mathcal J_k(x)}|\nabla u_t(x)|
                  +r_k^{-1}v_k(x)\right]\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}x
   \le C\left(D_Q+\sum_{q\in\mathcal R(Q)}a_q\right).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:prices}$$ All constants are absolute: the direction palette, support radius factor and cone aperture are fixed, and there is no dependence on $m$.

For every real $g\in W^{1,1}(\mathbb R^2)$ and $0<a\le b<\infty$, $S_{a,b}g$ is globally Lipschitz and $$\label{eq:band-lipschitz}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Lip}}(S_{a,b}g)\le rac{\|
abla g\|_1}{\pi a^2}.$$ If $b/a$ is a nonnegative integer power of two, then $$\label{eq:sobolev-band}
 \int_{\mathbb R^2}|
abla S_{a,b}g|\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm{d}x\le C\|
abla g\|_1,$$ where $C$ is absolute and independent of the band. This includes $a=b$, when the envelope is one average.
